/*
    `catch` handles an exception raised by `throw` in the left operand, using the handler
    function on the right.
    It responds to a thrown exception only. An expression that is merely undefined throws
    nothing, so `catch` does not apply - use `;;` for that case.
*/

(int n => n == 0 then throw "division by zero" else 100 / n) 0
    catch fn { string message -> 0 }
